[step] These exact picks lie on the raised identifiers or numeric/convention markings named in this part's manifest label windows. The font is Arial bold: 4.0 mm horizontal identifiers/conventions (4.3 mm for the basin title), or 3.6 mm tangent-aligned side angle values. Relief is nominally 0.48 mm, with the blade's side identifier projecting 0.49 mm. Their narrow readings include curved terminals and corners of letters, rather than nominal structural walls. The current emitted-width overlays in lettering-review.json identify these markings; physical legibility is unrecorded.
alternatives: Larger 4.8 mm glyphs or a stroke-expanded font can enlarge terminals, but need wider pads or revised side layouts to keep the full words and angle values on their backing. A separate larger label plate provides more room but adds another removable identity whose association with the sample must be maintained. Increasing relief enlarges the projecting side marks without enlarging their planar stroke widths.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/n08d-neck-cut-face-foot.step
click: x=2.548 y=-22.909 z=5.980

[sliver] These are the four sides of the 6.40 by 5.00 by 1.20 mm internal T-head on the 4.00 by 4.00 mm stem. The head's 1.20 mm X overhangs engage the keeper's matching lips, with 0.15 mm side air and 0.30 mm roof air. They retain the unmodified neck in its labelled holder without contacting its exterior show surface. Handling capacity and life are unmeasured.
alternatives: A 2.40 or 3.00 mm thick head provides more stock but requires a taller keeper cavity and roof and a new clearance/removal review. A larger head plan increases bearing area but also requires wider keeper slots in the confined source passage. A threaded tie adds hardware and contact with the source sample instead of this all-printed holder.
